{
  "_readme": "Hand-set level overrides, applied after the frequency ranking and the CEFR-J anchor.\n\n  pin       force to level 1 — greetings and politeness formulas.\n  beginner  force to level 1 — the closed semantic sets every A1 syllabus contains,\n            grouped by theme.\n  demote    force no higher than DEMOTE_FLOOR (B1).\n\nWHY `beginner` EXISTS. Corpus frequency cannot see concrete everyday vocabulary. Newspapers, encyclopedias, subtitles and novels almost never say fork, Thursday, purple or yogurt, so our ranking put `tenedor`, `zumo`, `verdura` and `yogur` at C2, `bolígrafo` and `morado` at C1, `manzana` and `cuchillo` at B2, and three of the seven weekdays at B1. Measured against an external A1 reference list, only 49% of a real beginner vocabulary was in our A1. No weighting fixes this — the words are genuinely rare in every corpus a computer can count; they are simply the first words a person learns.\n\nThese sets are written from knowledge of the languages, not copied from any graded list: the CATEGORIES (colours, weekdays, months, numbers, body, family, food, house, clothing, classroom) are universal syllabus structure, and every word is validated against our own CC BY-SA dictionary at build time. Third-party curated lists were deliberately not used — the good ones are either unlicensed or commercially copyrighted, the same reason CEFRLex and UniversalCEFR were declined.\n\nEvery entry must be a real dictionary headword; the build warns and skips anything that is not. A word in both `demote` and a pin list is a config error and the pin wins. See scripts/lib/coreOverrides.mjs.",
